WEBVTT

00:00.820 --> 00:03.010
Let's think about whether
each of these expressions

00:03.010 --> 00:06.022
produce rational or
irrational numbers.

00:06.022 --> 00:07.730
And just as a reminder,
a rational number

00:07.730 --> 00:10.680
is one-- so if you have
a rational number x,

00:10.680 --> 00:16.710
it can be expressed as the
ratio of two integers, m and n.

00:16.710 --> 00:21.140
And if you have an irrational
number, this cannot happen.

00:21.140 --> 00:24.120
So let's think
about each of these.

00:24.120 --> 00:26.250
So 9 is clearly a
rational number.

00:26.250 --> 00:31.230
You can express 9 as
9/1, 18/2, or 27/3.

00:31.230 --> 00:35.410
So it can clearly be expressed
as the ratio of two integers.

00:35.410 --> 00:37.445
But what about the
square root of 45?

00:37.445 --> 00:39.070
So let's think about
that a little bit.

00:39.070 --> 00:41.530
Square root of 45.

00:41.530 --> 00:45.310
That's the same thing as the
square root of 9 times 5,

00:45.310 --> 00:47.180
which is the same thing
as the square root

00:47.180 --> 00:50.430
of 9 times the square root of 5.

00:50.430 --> 00:52.730
The principal root
of 9 is 3, so it's

00:52.730 --> 00:54.960
3 times the square root of 5.

00:54.960 --> 00:59.420
So this is going to be 9 plus
3 times the square root of 5.

00:59.420 --> 01:02.000
So the square root
of 5 is irrational.

01:02.000 --> 01:05.280
You're taking the square root
of a non-perfect square right

01:05.280 --> 01:06.100
over here.

01:06.100 --> 01:07.350
Irrational.

01:07.350 --> 01:10.395
3 is rational, but the product
of a rational and an irrational

01:10.395 --> 01:12.060
is still going to be irrational.

01:12.060 --> 01:13.750
So that's going
to be irrational.

01:13.750 --> 01:15.630
And then you're taking
an irrational number

01:15.630 --> 01:17.030
and you're adding 9 to it.

01:17.030 --> 01:18.760
You're adding a
rational number to it.

01:18.760 --> 01:21.800
But you add a rational
to an irrational,

01:21.800 --> 01:24.040
and you're still going
to have an irrational.

01:24.040 --> 01:26.530
So this whole thing
is irrational.

01:29.500 --> 01:34.970
Now let's think about this
expression right over here.

01:34.970 --> 01:36.470
Well, the numerator
can be rewritten

01:36.470 --> 01:40.400
as the square root of 9 times
5 over 3 times the square root

01:40.400 --> 01:41.320
of 5.

01:41.320 --> 01:43.236
Well, that's the same
thing as the square root

01:43.236 --> 01:47.300
of 9 times the square root of
5 over 3 times the square root

01:47.300 --> 01:48.230
of 5.

01:48.230 --> 01:52.200
Well, that's the same thing as
3 times the square root of 5

01:52.200 --> 01:55.420
over 3 times the
square root of 5.

01:55.420 --> 01:57.890
Well, that's just
going to be equal to 1.

01:57.890 --> 01:59.350
Or you could view it as 1/1.

01:59.350 --> 02:01.430
And 1 is clearly
a rational number.

02:01.430 --> 02:05.880
You could write it as 1/1,
2/2, 3/3, really any integer

02:05.880 --> 02:07.070
over itself.

02:07.070 --> 02:11.245
So this is going to be rational.

02:11.245 --> 02:13.370
Now, let's do this last
expression right over here.

02:13.370 --> 02:15.440
3 times the principal root of 9.

02:15.440 --> 02:17.280
Well, what's the
principal root of 9?

02:17.280 --> 02:18.000
Well, it's 3.

02:18.000 --> 02:21.490
So this is going to be 3
times 3, which is equal to 9.

02:21.490 --> 02:23.280
And we've already
talked about the fact

02:23.280 --> 02:25.880
that 9 can clearly be
expressed as the ratio of two

02:25.880 --> 02:34.933
integers-- 9/1, 27/3, 45/5, all
different ways of expressing 9.
